WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.796 --> 00:00:08.795
David Barron: Okay, I think the recording's on. Hi, this is CS 3604 virtual office hours for Wednesday the nineteenth.

2
00:00:09.124 --> 00:00:13.976
David Barron: I record these so people who can't make it can watch later.

3
00:00:14.313 --> 00:00:21.184
David Barron: If you'd rather not be on the recording, just keep your camera off and ask in the chat.

4
00:00:21.665 --> 00:00:27.191
David Barron: Alex, I see you in the waiting room, let me admit you. Hey Alex.

5
00:00:31.512 --> 00:00:36.184
Alex R.: Hey, um, can you hear me? My mic's been weird all day.

6
00:00:37.353 --> 00:00:41.488
David Barron: Yeah, you're a little quiet but I can hear you.

7
00:00:42.908 --> 00:00:47.027
Alex R.: Okay, cool. So I had a question about P3, the grade.

8
00:00:47.490 --> 00:00:55.218
Alex R.: I got the feedback yesterday and I lost, uh, four points on the stakeholder section and I wasn't totally sure why.

9
00:00:56.792 --> 00:01:05.860
David Barron: Sure, let me pull it up. One sec. Okay. So P3 is the case analysis, and for the stakeholder section the rubric has three rows.

10
00:01:06.353 --> 00:01:17.801
David Barron: You need at least four stakeholders, what each one stands to gain or lose, and then you connect at least two of them to a specific principle in the ACM Code.

11
00:01:19.514 --> 00:01:21.931
Alex R.: Right, I thought I did that.

12
00:01:22.617 --> 00:01:25.427
David Barron: You did the first two really well.

13
00:01:25.501 --> 00:01:33.662
David Barron: The thing is, you cite the ACM Code in general, like "this violates the code", but you never point to a principle.

14
00:01:33.866 --> 00:01:43.474
David Barron: So that row is where the four points went. If you'd said, like, 1.6, respect privacy, or 2.5 on evaluating risks, that would've been full credit.

15
00:01:44.824 --> 00:01:49.436
Alex R.: Oh. Okay, that's fair. Is there any way to, like, fix it?

16
00:01:50.828 --> 00:01:59.035
David Barron: There's no resubmission for P3, sorry. But it's the same rubric row on the final project, so I'd just carry it forward.

17
00:01:59.168 --> 00:02:07.848
David Barron: Honestly that's the most common thing I took points off for. I think something like a third of the class did the same thing.

18
00:02:08.658 --> 00:02:10.713
Alex R.: Okay, good to know. Thanks.

19
00:02:12.271 --> 00:02:13.627
David Barron: Yep. Anything else?

20
00:02:14.526 --> 00:02:17.164
Alex R.: No, that was it. Thanks, David.

21
00:02:17.758 --> 00:02:24.027
David Barron: Cool, see you. Okay, Jasmine and Marco, you both came in, welcome. Who wants to go first?

22
00:02:24.803 --> 00:02:30.761
Jasmine T.: I can go, mine's quick. For P4, the policy memo, is the two-page limit including references?

23
00:02:39.486 --> 00:02:46.070
David Barron: No, references don't count. Two pages of body, eleven-point font, and then references on a separate page.

24
00:02:46.131 --> 00:02:54.343
David Barron: The appendix doesn't count either, but please don't put actual arguments in the appendix. I'll read it but I won't grade it.

25
00:02:54.885 --> 00:03:01.444
Jasmine T.: Got it. And can we use the case from P3 again, or does it have to be new?

26
00:03:02.801 --> 00:03:05.514
David Barron: It has to be a different case.

27
00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:16.509
David Barron: You can stay in the same area, like if you did facial recognition for P3 you could do a different surveillance policy, but it can't be the same incident.

28
00:03:17.814 --> 00:03:19.497
Jasmine T.: Okay, cool, that helps.

29
00:03:28.046 --> 00:03:30.905
Marco D.: Um, can I ask about late days?

30
00:03:31.475 --> 00:03:32.943
David Barron: Yeah, go ahead.

31
00:03:41.309 --> 00:03:47.919
Marco D.: So I think I have one late day left, and P4 is due Friday at eleven fifty-nine.

32
00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:53.678
Marco D.: If I submit at like twelve-oh-five, does that count as a whole late day?

33
00:03:54.728 --> 00:03:59.638
David Barron: So the policy in the syllabus is that late days are whole days.

34
00:04:00.101 --> 00:04:06.115
David Barron: Anything after eleven fifty-nine counts as one late day, so twelve-oh-five would use your last one.

35
00:04:07.019 --> 00:04:09.369
Marco D.: Even if it's five minutes?

36
00:04:15.969 --> 00:04:22.189
David Barron: Yeah. Canvas marks it late and I go by the timestamp. I know it feels harsh.

37
00:04:22.375 --> 00:04:27.365
David Barron: There's no grace period written in, at least not that I know of.

38
00:04:28.243 --> 00:04:33.905
Marco D.: Because last week someone in my section said there was, like, a fifteen-minute grace thing.

39
00:04:35.474 --> 00:04:44.883
David Barron: Hmm. I don't think so? Let me check with the instructor and I'll post the answer in the Canvas discussion so everyone gets the same answer.

40
00:04:45.159 --> 00:04:50.251
David Barron: I don't want to tell you one thing and then grade another way.

41
00:04:56.177 --> 00:05:00.435
Marco D.: Okay, yeah, please. And if I've already used all of them?

42
00:05:01.816 --> 00:05:11.696
David Barron: Then it's ten percent off per day after that, up to three days, and after three days it's a zero unless you have an approved extension.

43
00:05:11.966 --> 00:05:17.356
David Barron: So if you're out of late days, just submit what you have on time.

44
00:05:18.395 --> 00:05:19.495
Marco D.: Okay. Thanks.

45
00:05:20.485 --> 00:05:24.503
Jasmine T.: Wait, sorry, can I ask one more thing about P3?

46
00:05:25.720 --> 00:05:26.820
David Barron: Sure.

47
00:05:27.497 --> 00:05:32.258
Jasmine T.: The feedback said my recommendation was "underspecified". What does that mean exactly?

48
00:05:32.917 --> 00:05:39.933
David Barron: Yeah, so the recommendation row wants something someone could actually do. "The company should be more ethical" is underspecified.

49
00:05:40.065 --> 00:05:48.393
David Barron: "The company should require an independent audit before deploying the model in a new city" is specific. Who does what, and by when.

50
00:05:56.546 --> 00:05:58.441
Jasmine T.: Ah, okay. That makes sense.

51
00:05:58.998 --> 00:06:01.777
David Barron: Hana, I see you just joined. Hi.

52
00:06:03.154 --> 00:06:09.356
Hana K.: Hi! Sorry, I was in another meeting. Um, I had a question about final project teams.

53
00:06:10.314 --> 00:06:11.414
David Barron: Sure.

54
00:06:12.350 --> 00:06:20.889
Hana K.: Our team has five people now because someone joined from another team that split up, and the spec says four. Is that okay?

55
00:06:22.109 --> 00:06:31.372
David Barron: Five is fine if it's because of a team change. Put all five names on the proposal and add a sentence on who's doing what.

56
00:06:31.736 --> 00:06:35.225
David Barron: I'd also email the instructor so it's on record.

57
00:06:36.168 --> 00:06:39.453
Hana K.: Okay. And the proposal's due December first, right?

58
00:06:40.530 --> 00:06:41.952
David Barron: December first, yeah.

59
00:06:42.332 --> 00:06:52.242
David Barron: The final project itself is due the tenth, and I'm grading all sixty-four of those, so, um, please, please label your sections the way the rubric does.

60
00:06:52.379 --> 00:06:55.408
David Barron: It makes my life so much easier.

61
00:06:56.331 --> 00:06:57.906
Hana K.: Haha, okay, will do.

62
00:06:59.233 --> 00:07:04.913
Marco D.: Do you know when P3 grades will be final? Like if someone wants a regrade?

63
00:07:05.627 --> 00:07:12.470
David Barron: Regrade requests are open for one week after grades come out, so for P3 that's until next Tuesday.

64
00:07:12.962 --> 00:07:18.434
David Barron: Use the form linked in the syllabus, not email. Say which rubric row and why.

65
00:07:24.004 --> 00:07:25.109
Marco D.: Got it.

66
00:07:26.085 --> 00:07:33.606
David Barron: Okay, anyone else? No? I'll stay on another ten minutes in case someone shows up. Otherwise see you all Friday.

67
00:07:41.361 --> 00:07:42.461
Jasmine T.: Thanks!

68
00:07:48.324 --> 00:07:49.424
Hana K.: Thank you!

69
00:07:50.748 --> 00:07:55.090
David Barron: Okay, nobody else came, I'm going to stop the recording. Bye.
